{:edb/db-before-t 227 :edb/db-after-t 228 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533540 50 #inst "2026-10-11T05:50:22.834Z" 13194139533540 17592186045585 true] [17592186124444 1038 #edb/ref 17592186407348 13194139533540 17592186045585 true] [17592186125189 1068 #edb/ref 17592186123704 13194139533540 17592186045585 false] [17592186125189 1068 #edb/ref 17592186407346 13194139533540 17592186045585 true] [17592186125189 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533540 17592186045585 true] [17592186125189 1071 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S$, defined by $z = f(x, y)$, with the plane $x = 1$. Given that the point $P(1,-1, 2)$ lies on $C$, and that $f_{y} = x^{2}(2xy - 1)$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$?" 13194139533540 17592186045585 false] [17592186125189 1071 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=1.$ Given that the point $P(1,-1,2)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}={x}^{2}(2xy-1)$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" 13194139533540 17592186045585 true] [17592186125189 1074 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(1,-1).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,-1) & =(1)^{2}(2(1)(-1)-1)=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(1,-1) & ={f}_{y}(1,-1)(y+1) \\\\ z-2 & =-3(y+1) \\\\ z-2 & =-3y-3 \\\\ z & =-3y-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,-1,2)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ z=-3y-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=1,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=-3y-1$ (or $-3y-z-1=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,-3,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,-3,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =j-3\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $j-3\\,k.$" 13194139533540 17592186045585 true] [17592186407335 1043 #edb/ref 17592186407349 13194139533540 17592186045585 true] [17592186407335 1043 #edb/ref 17592186407350 13194139533540 17592186045585 true] [17592186407335 1043 #edb/ref 17592186407351 13194139533540 17592186045585 true] [17592186407335 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533540 17592186045585 true] [17592186407335 1045 "Geometric Interpretations of Partial Derivatives" 13194139533540 17592186045585 true] [17592186407335 1046 #edb/ref 17592186407350 13194139533540 17592186045585 true] [17592186407335 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046665 13194139533540 17592186045585 true] [17592186407335 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050164 13194139533540 17592186045585 true] [17592186407335 1053 #uuid "f13d4530-1c8e-598a-986b-9b8f2a13e396" 13194139533540 17592186045585 true] [17592186407336 1081 "j-3\\,k" 13194139533540 17592186045585 true] [17592186407336 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533540 17592186045585 true] [17592186407336 1084 #uuid "25cf98fa-a5b3-5e6b-b710-84fa1db64ec9" 13194139533540 17592186045585 true] [17592186407337 1081 "{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ z=-y+1\\end{aligned}" 13194139533540 17592186045585 true] [17592186407337 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533540 17592186045585 true] [17592186407337 1084 #uuid "3f49be2f-9fbd-57d9-8743-c7f792e14929" 13194139533540 17592186045585 true] [17592186407338 1081 "{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ z=-2y-1\\end{aligned}" 13194139533540 17592186045585 true] [17592186407338 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533540 17592186045585 true] [17592186407338 1084 #uuid "40205211-9c82-5bbc-b29c-5211d1a45bbb" 1